1
00:00:00,093 --> 00:00:02,845
A neural network is a long chain of functions.

2
00:00:03,284 --> 00:00:10,664
The input goes into the first layer, its output goes into the second layer, and so on, until the loss at the end.

3
00:00:11,077 --> 00:00:16,768
To train the network, we need the slope of the loss with respect to a weight deep inside that chain.

4
00:00:17,194 --> 00:00:19,215
The tool for this is the chain rule.

5
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:22,539
Its message is short: slopes multiply.

6
00:00:23,473 --> 00:00:28,765
In this video you will learn to see a function inside a function as a pipeline.

7
00:00:29,177 --> 00:00:34,257
You will see why the slopes multiply, using local linearity twice.

8
00:00:34,696 --> 00:00:41,930
You will work through examples with numbers, meet the Jacobian, the matrix version, and preview backpropagation.

9
00:00:42,356 --> 00:00:46,824
You need video seven on the derivative, and video eight on the gradient.

10
00:00:48,573 --> 00:00:50,886
Let's start with a pipeline of two machines.

11
00:00:51,325 --> 00:00:54,503
The first machine, f, takes x and gives u.

12
00:00:54,942 --> 00:00:58,799
The second machine, g, takes u and gives y.

13
00:00:59,397 --> 00:01:03,054
Putting one function after another like this is called composition.

14
00:01:03,493 --> 00:01:05,953
We write it as g of f of x.

15
00:01:06,899 --> 00:01:08,322
Here is our example.

16
00:01:08,761 --> 00:01:12,923
The inner function is f of x equals three x plus one.

17
00:01:13,362 --> 00:01:16,514
The outer function is g of u equals u squared.

18
00:01:16,952 --> 00:01:18,521
Put in x equals one.

19
00:01:18,947 --> 00:01:22,511
The inner machine gives three plus one, which is four.

20
00:01:22,950 --> 00:01:26,248
The outer machine gives four squared, which is sixteen.

21
00:01:26,673 --> 00:01:28,535
So g of f of one is sixteen.

22
00:01:29,473 --> 00:01:30,231
Order matters.

23
00:01:30,643 --> 00:01:36,574
If we apply g first and then f, we get three times x squared, plus one.

24
00:01:37,012 --> 00:01:40,935
At x equals one, that is four, not sixteen.

25
00:01:41,374 --> 00:01:45,164
So g of f and f of g are different functions.

26
00:01:45,603 --> 00:01:48,156
Always read a composition from the inside out.

27
00:01:50,473 --> 00:01:52,879
Now, how fast does the output change?

28
00:01:53,345 --> 00:01:54,568
Think of three gears.

29
00:01:54,994 --> 00:01:59,648
When the first gear turns once, the second gear turns three times.

30
00:02:00,074 --> 00:02:04,728
When the second gear turns once, the third gear turns two times.

31
00:02:05,180 --> 00:02:08,132
How fast does the third gear turn compared with the first?

32
00:02:09,073 --> 00:02:12,450
One turn of the first gear makes three turns of the second.

33
00:02:12,889 --> 00:02:15,190
Each of those makes two turns of the third.

34
00:02:15,615 --> 00:02:20,110
So the third gear turns three times two, which is six times as fast.

35
00:02:20,535 --> 00:02:21,865
The rates multiply.

36
00:02:22,304 --> 00:02:23,168
They do not add.

37
00:02:24,746 --> 00:02:26,262
Functions work the same way.

38
00:02:26,674 --> 00:02:34,573
The slope of y with respect to x equals the slope of y with respect to u, times the slope of u with respect to x.

39
00:02:34,998 --> 00:02:36,328
This is called the chain rule.

40
00:02:36,740 --> 00:02:40,557
It looks like fractions cancelling, and that is a good way to remember it.

41
00:02:42,272 --> 00:02:42,990
Why is it true?

42
00:02:43,416 --> 00:02:47,950
We can derive it from local linearity, axiom C, used twice.

43
00:02:48,389 --> 00:02:51,195
Step one: nudge x by a small step h.

44
00:02:51,634 --> 00:02:55,543
The inner function changes by about f prime of x times h.

45
00:02:55,822 --> 00:02:57,445
Call that small change k.

46
00:02:58,339 --> 00:03:03,152
Step two: the outer function sees its input u change by k.

47
00:03:03,764 --> 00:03:08,897
Local linearity again: g changes by about g prime of u times k.

48
00:03:09,323 --> 00:03:10,067
Now put in k.

49
00:03:10,506 --> 00:03:15,785
The output changes by g prime of u, times f prime of x, times h.

50
00:03:16,725 --> 00:03:24,199
So the slope of the whole pipeline is g prime of u times f prime of x, where u is f of x.

51
00:03:24,638 --> 00:03:25,728
That is the chain rule.

52
00:03:26,140 --> 00:03:30,994
Each machine, close up, just multiplies small changes by its slope.

53
00:03:31,419 --> 00:03:35,528
Two multiplications in a row are one multiplication by the product.

54
00:03:37,239 --> 00:03:42,930
Let's compute the slope of three x plus one, squared, at x equals one.

55
00:03:43,356 --> 00:03:48,249
The inner slope: the derivative of three x plus one is three.

56
00:03:48,675 --> 00:03:52,451
The outer slope: the derivative of u squared is two u.

57
00:03:52,890 --> 00:03:54,114
Now the important part.

58
00:03:54,539 --> 00:03:58,223
We evaluate it at the inner value, u equals four.

59
00:03:58,661 --> 00:04:00,403
So the outer slope is eight.

60
00:04:01,339 --> 00:04:04,730
Multiply: eight times three is twenty four.

61
00:04:05,115 --> 00:04:09,956
So the slope of the composition at x equals one is twenty four.

62
00:04:10,394 --> 00:04:11,924
Watch out for a common mistake.

63
00:04:12,363 --> 00:04:16,019
Do not evaluate the outer slope at x equals one.

64
00:04:16,445 --> 00:04:19,477
That would give two, and a wrong answer of six.

65
00:04:21,005 --> 00:04:22,202
Let's check in two ways.

66
00:04:22,694 --> 00:04:27,774
First, expand the square: nine x squared, plus six x, plus one.

67
00:04:28,186 --> 00:04:31,112
Its derivative is eighteen x plus six.

68
00:04:31,537 --> 00:04:33,944
At x equals one, that is twenty four.

69
00:04:34,383 --> 00:04:38,758
Second, a numeric check with a tiny step also gives twenty four.

70
00:04:39,183 --> 00:04:40,473
All three answers agree.

71
00:04:42,205 --> 00:04:43,176
Chains can be longer.

72
00:04:43,615 --> 00:04:44,798
Take three steps.

73
00:04:45,264 --> 00:04:47,378
First, a equals two x.

74
00:04:47,790 --> 00:04:49,147
Then b equals a squared.

75
00:04:49,572 --> 00:04:51,234
Then c equals five b.

76
00:04:51,647 --> 00:04:53,455
Each step has its own local slope.

77
00:04:53,867 --> 00:05:01,048
At x equals one, a is two, and the slopes are two, then two a, which is four, and then five.

78
00:05:01,972 --> 00:05:07,850
The chain rule simply multiplies all of them: two times four times five equals forty.

79
00:05:08,288 --> 00:05:14,312
Check it directly: c equals five times two x, squared, which is twenty x squared.

80
00:05:14,738 --> 00:05:19,844
Its derivative is forty x, and at x equals one that is forty.

81
00:05:20,256 --> 00:05:20,908
Correct again.

82
00:05:22,392 --> 00:05:24,772
It does not matter how long the chain is.

83
00:05:25,198 --> 00:05:30,730
Ten steps or a hundred steps, you multiply the local slopes along the way.

84
00:05:31,169 --> 00:05:36,262
This is exactly what happens inside a deep neural network with many layers.

85
00:05:38,005 --> 00:05:41,755
What if a machine has several inputs and several outputs?

86
00:05:42,194 --> 00:05:44,082
Then one slope is not enough.

87
00:05:44,508 --> 00:05:49,667
We need the partial derivative of every output with respect to every input.

88
00:05:50,106 --> 00:05:55,239
Put them in a grid: one row for each output, one column for each input.

89
00:05:55,678 --> 00:05:57,846
This matrix is called the Jacobian.

90
00:05:59,405 --> 00:06:07,158
Example: F takes x one and x two, and outputs x one squared, and x one times x two.

91
00:06:07,583 --> 00:06:13,621
The first row holds the partial derivatives of x one squared: two x one, and zero.

92
00:06:14,046 --> 00:06:19,166
The second row holds those of x one times x two: x two, and x one.

93
00:06:19,605 --> 00:06:24,591
At the point one, two, the Jacobian is two, zero, two, one.

94
00:06:25,492 --> 00:06:32,367
A nice fact: for a linear mapping, x goes to A times x, the Jacobian is just the matrix A.

95
00:06:32,806 --> 00:06:36,516
So the Jacobian is the local linear mapping of the function.

96
00:06:36,941 --> 00:06:40,519
Close up, every smooth function acts like a matrix.

97
00:06:42,039 --> 00:06:44,592
Now the chain rule for many dimensions.

98
00:06:45,004 --> 00:06:51,148
Chained functions have chained Jacobians, and the Jacobians multiply, as matrices.

99
00:06:51,573 --> 00:06:55,789
Let G take u one and u two and give u one plus u two squared.

100
00:06:56,227 --> 00:07:01,573
Its Jacobian at F of one, two, which is one, two, is the row one, four.

101
00:07:02,479 --> 00:07:06,162
Multiply the row one, four by the Jacobian of F.

102
00:07:06,601 --> 00:07:09,128
The result is the row ten, four.

103
00:07:09,566 --> 00:07:10,364
Check directly.

104
00:07:10,803 --> 00:07:16,136
G of F is x one squared plus x one squared times x two squared.

105
00:07:16,548 --> 00:07:20,351
Its partial derivatives at one, two are ten and four.

106
00:07:20,790 --> 00:07:22,931
The matrix product gives the right answer.

107
00:07:24,672 --> 00:07:25,829
Now machine learning.

108
00:07:26,228 --> 00:07:33,262
A network is a chain: input, layer one, layer two, and so on, then the loss.

109
00:07:33,688 --> 00:07:41,161
The book on building large language models describes backpropagation as an implementation of the chain rule for neural networks.

110
00:07:41,600 --> 00:07:46,414
Backpropagation is a molecule built from this atom, so here we only preview it.

111
00:07:47,339 --> 00:07:49,107
The key trick is the direction.

112
00:07:49,546 --> 00:07:54,413
Start at the end, where the slope of the loss with respect to itself is one.

113
00:07:54,839 --> 00:07:58,708
Walk backward, and multiply by each local slope.

114
00:07:59,134 --> 00:08:06,847
In our three step chain: one, then times five is five, then times four is twenty, then times two is forty.

115
00:08:07,759 --> 00:08:12,572
Each product is computed once and reused for everything further back.

116
00:08:13,025 --> 00:08:17,120
That makes the backward pass fast, even with millions of weights.

117
00:08:17,546 --> 00:08:20,684
Libraries like PyTorch do this automatically.

118
00:08:21,096 --> 00:08:26,083
You only need to remember the idea: slopes multiply, from the end backward.

119
00:08:27,805 --> 00:08:28,696
Now it is your turn.

120
00:08:29,135 --> 00:08:30,970
Pause the video and try this.

121
00:08:31,396 --> 00:08:36,183
What is the slope of two x minus one, cubed, at x equals one?

122
00:08:36,635 --> 00:08:41,595
Find the inner slope, then the outer slope at the inner value, and multiply.

123
00:08:42,519 --> 00:08:43,689
Here is the answer.

124
00:08:44,128 --> 00:08:47,705
The inner function two x minus one has slope two.

125
00:08:48,130 --> 00:08:51,561
At x equals one, the inner value is one.

126
00:08:51,960 --> 00:08:57,519
The outer function u cubed has slope three u squared, which is three at u equals one.

127
00:08:57,957 --> 00:09:01,202
So the slope is three times two, which is six.

128
00:09:02,705 --> 00:09:03,477
One more question.

129
00:09:03,902 --> 00:09:08,370
Let a equal x squared, and b equal three a plus one.

130
00:09:08,796 --> 00:09:12,665
What is the slope of b with respect to x at x equals two?

131
00:09:13,091 --> 00:09:13,782
Pause and try.

132
00:09:14,705 --> 00:09:19,838
The slope of a with respect to x is two x, which is four at x equals two.

133
00:09:20,264 --> 00:09:22,857
The slope of b with respect to a is three.

134
00:09:23,282 --> 00:09:26,261
Multiply: three times four is twelve.

135
00:09:26,687 --> 00:09:33,455
Check: b is three x squared plus one, with derivative six x, which is twelve at two.

136
00:09:35,172 --> 00:09:36,329
Let's check this in Python.

137
00:09:36,768 --> 00:09:38,350
We write a numeric slope function.

138
00:09:38,789 --> 00:09:44,374
Then we compare the chain rule with a numeric slope for three x plus one, squared.

139
00:09:44,786 --> 00:09:49,002
We multiply the local slopes of the three step chain from the end backward.

140
00:09:49,427 --> 00:09:52,512
And we multiply two Jacobians with NumPy.

141
00:09:54,039 --> 00:09:58,174
This output is real; the script ran while the video was made.

142
00:09:58,600 --> 00:10:03,001
Eight times three is twenty four, and the numeric slope agrees.

143
00:10:03,427 --> 00:10:06,765
The backward products are five, twenty, and forty.

144
00:10:07,177 --> 00:10:13,281
And the product of the Jacobians is ten, four, the same as the numeric check.

145
00:10:15,005 --> 00:10:15,657
In summary.

146
00:10:16,096 --> 00:10:21,401
A composition puts one function after another, g of f of x.

147
00:10:21,854 --> 00:10:28,808
The chain rule says the slopes multiply: the outer slope, at the inner value, times the inner slope.

148
00:10:29,234 --> 00:10:32,093
It comes from local linearity used twice.

149
00:10:32,505 --> 00:10:34,859
Longer chains multiply more slopes.

150
00:10:35,805 --> 00:10:43,465
With many inputs and outputs, the slopes form a Jacobian, and chained Jacobians multiply as matrices.

151
00:10:43,904 --> 00:10:48,026
Backpropagation applies the chain rule from the end of the network backward.

152
00:10:48,465 --> 00:10:54,954
Next, in video ten: the exponential and the logarithm, which turn products into sums.

153
00:10:55,380 --> 00:10:55,938
See you there.

154
